Architecture
============

Startup
-------

App.js does four things in order and nothing else:

1. Loads the fonts. React Native needs each weight registered as its own
   family, so every Space Grotesk and Inter variant is named explicitly. The
   keys here must match the strings in src/theme/typography.js.
2. Holds the native splash until fonts are ready, so there is never a flash of
   the system font.
3. Mounts the provider tree.
4. Hands off to AppShell.

	<SafeAreaProvider>
	  <View>                          root, appBg
	    <AuthProvider>                who is signed in, tokens
	      <PushGate />                registers the Expo push token
	      <AppProvider>               player, quests, challenges, badges
	        <AppShell />              chooses the auth stack or the app stack
	      </AppProvider>
	    </AuthProvider>
	  </View>
	</SafeAreaProvider>

The order matters. PushGate must sit inside AuthProvider because a device
token has to attach to a signed-in account, and AppProvider must sit inside
AuthProvider because every fetch it makes needs a token.


Routing
-------

src/components/AppShell.js reads the auth state and renders one of two
navigators.

AuthNavigator (src/navigation/AuthNavigator.js), a native stack:

	signin, signup, verifyEmail, forgot, forgotVerify, resetPassword

AppNavigator (src/navigation/AppNavigator.js), a native stack whose first
screen is the tab navigator:

	Stack:  Tabs, Badges, Stats, ExerciseStats, avatarJourney
	Tabs:   home, quests, log, board, profile

Tabs render through a custom tab bar (src/components/TabBar.js) with the
raised gold action button in the centre.

Anything that is a destination rather than a section belongs in the stack, not
the tabs. Badges and Stats were tabs early on and were moved for that reason.


State
-----

Two contexts, no store library.

src/context/AuthContext.js
	The signed-in user, isAuthenticated, isLoading, and the sign-in,
	sign-up and sign-out actions. Owns the token lifecycle together with
	services/tokenStore.js. Logout also unregisters the push token.

src/context/AppContext.js
	Everything the signed-in experience reads: player profile, today's quests,
	challenges, which challenges the user joined, per-challenge submission
	status, badges earned and the badge catalogue, the queue of newly awarded
	badges awaiting a celebration modal, level-up state, the toast, and the
	currently open bottom sheet.

	It is the fetch orchestrator as well as the store. Screens call actions on
	it rather than calling services directly, which is what keeps a challenge
	joined on one screen from going stale on another.

Screen-local state stays in the screen. Anything two screens must agree on
goes in AppContext.


Data flow
---------

	AppContext action  -> src/services/<x>Api.js
	                   -> src/services/apiClient.js
	                   -> fetch, Bearer token, one refresh retry on 401
	                   -> state update
	                   -> screens rerender

	socket event       -> src/services/realtime.js
	                   -> refetch through the same AppContext action
	                   -> optional local banner

	push notification  -> expo-notifications
	                   -> banner while the app is closed or backgrounded

Sockets and push are complementary. Sockets update a running app; push reaches
a closed one. Both are fired by the same backend controller.

There is no cache and no optimistic update. After a mutation the client
refetches, because XP, standings and validation state can all be changed by
staff while the screen is open.


Local persistence
-----------------

AsyncStorage, behind three small stores. Nothing else in the app touches
AsyncStorage directly.

	services/tokenStore.js      access and refresh tokens
	services/pinStore.js        which exercises are pinned to the home widget
	services/settingsStore.js   quest reminders and auto check-in toggles

settingsStore keeps an in-memory cache with a subscribe function so a toggle
takes effect immediately rather than on the next read.


Device capabilities
-------------------

	hooks/usePedometer.js         step counts and the weekly history, via
	                              expo-sensors
	hooks/useGymProximity.js      expo-location; a rising-edge crossing into
	                              the TLC radius prompts a check-in once,
	                              not repeatedly
	hooks/usePushNotifications.js registers the Expo push token when
	                              authenticated

All three degrade quietly when a permission is refused. None of them may block
a render.